# Line endings.
#
# `npm run generate` writes the content_scripts block into manifest.json with
# LF, while a Windows checkout under core.autocrlf=true gives the rest of the
# file CRLF. The result is a manifest with mixed line endings, which hid a real
# defect: scripts/lib/manifest-splice.mjs round-trips LF but not CRLF, so
# `tests/manifest-splice.test.js` ("splicing an unchanged value back is
# byte-for-byte identical") passes on a mixed or LF file and fails on a
# uniformly-CRLF one — i.e. on any fresh Windows clone.
#
# Pinning the text formats the tooling reads and writes to LF makes every
# checkout byte-identical on every platform, which is what the byte-for-byte
# test is actually asserting.
*.js    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf

# Binaries are left to git's own detection.
